# Jinja Render Test for Notification / Email Template Fixtures

When shipping Frappe Notification or Email Template fixtures, fixture-import assertions (`frappe.db.exists("Notification", name)`) are **not enough**. They only prove the row imported — they do not exercise the Jinja templates.

## Why

In Phase 4 of the MY Schools ERP, three Notification templates referenced fields that did not exist on their target doctypes (`doc.total_royalty`, `doc.title`, `doc.description`). All fixture-import tests passed because **Frappe defers Jinja evaluation until the alert fires.** Every alert would have crashed at send time in production. The bugs were caught only after Ali pushed back with *"have you tested it from all perspectives?"* — see [`pr-verification`](pr-verification.mdc).

## How to apply

For every Notification / Email Template fixture, add a regression test that:

1. Builds an **unsaved** stub doc with `frappe.get_doc(<dict of sample fields>)` for the target doctype — no DB hit, no save, no validation.
2. Calls `frappe.utils.jinja.render_template(notif.subject, {"doc": doc})` and the same for `notif.message`.
3. Asserts the rendered output contains no unrendered `{{` markers AND includes at least one real attribute value (e.g. branch name or doc name).

## Reference implementation

Lives in `frappe-bench/apps/myschools/myschools/tests/test_notifications.py` under `TestNotificationJinjaRenders`. Use the same `SAMPLE_DOCS: ClassVar[dict]` pattern for new fixtures.
